WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.493 --> 00:00:02.140
- There is no one who doesn't

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.125
have obstacles in their life.

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:06.273
Even Bill Gates has obstacles.

00:00:06.273 --> 00:00:07.124
He's got to figure out

00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:08.233
how to keep all that money.

00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:09.095
Why is it that so many

00:00:09.095 --> 00:00:10.861
people act like animals?

00:00:10.861 --> 00:00:11.978
Why do they just react

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.255
to what's going on

00:00:13.255 --> 00:00:15.187
rather than to plan and strategize?

00:00:15.187 --> 00:00:17.321
Because if you don't accept excuses

00:00:17.321 --> 00:00:19.096
pretty soon people stop giving them,

00:00:19.096 --> 00:00:20.839
and they start looking for solutions.

00:00:20.839 --> 00:00:21.696
There's no such thing

00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:23.916
as useless knowledge because

00:00:23.916 --> 00:00:25.275
you never know what doors

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:27.341
it's going to open for you.

00:00:27.341 --> 00:00:29.005
Almost any accomplishment

00:00:29.005 --> 00:00:30.994
in the history of the world

00:00:30.994 --> 00:00:33.027
has come at the hands of

00:00:33.027 --> 00:00:34.673
people who have taken risks.

00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:35.506
Some people hate spiders.

00:00:35.506 --> 00:00:36.573
Some people hate snakes.

00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:39.102
I hated poverty I couldn't stand it.

00:00:39.102 --> 00:00:41.174
If you have an average brain

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:44.179
you're capable of almost anything.

00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:45.914
Be nice to people because

00:00:45.914 --> 00:00:47.382
once they get over their suspicion

00:00:47.382 --> 00:00:48.215
of why you're being nice

00:00:48.215 --> 00:00:49.308
they'll be nice to you.

00:00:49.308 --> 00:00:51.601
Real success involves lifting up

00:00:51.601 --> 00:00:53.417
those who are around you.

00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:55.842
- He's a retired American neurosurgeon,

00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:57.727
and former presidential candidate

00:00:57.727 --> 00:00:59.053
of the United States.

00:00:59.053 --> 00:01:00.424
He has authored numerous books on

00:01:00.424 --> 00:01:01.438
his medical career,

00:01:01.438 --> 00:01:02.897
and political stances.

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:03.789
He was a Director of

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:05.224
Pediatric Neurosurgery at

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:07.804
the John Hopkins Hospital in Maryland.

00:01:07.804 --> 00:01:08.693
He's Ben Carson,

00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:09.795
and here is my take on

00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:12.212
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:16.080
- You know, I'm reminded of a very

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.869
successful young businessman.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:20.726
He loved to buy his mother

00:01:20.726 --> 00:01:23.049
these exotic gifts for Mother's Day.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.300
He ran out of ideals,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.525
and then he ran across these birds.

00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:28.127
These birds were cool, you know,

00:01:28.127 --> 00:01:29.781
they cost $5,000 apiece.

00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:31.369
They could dance, they could sing,

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:32.202
they could talk to you.

00:01:32.202 --> 00:01:33.679
He was so excited he bought two of them,

00:01:33.679 --> 00:01:35.229
sent them to his mother,

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:36.378
couldn't wait to call her up

00:01:36.378 --> 00:01:37.211
on Mother's Day.

00:01:37.211 --> 00:01:38.044
"Mother, mother, what did you

00:01:38.044 --> 00:01:38.877
"think of those birds?"

00:01:38.877 --> 00:01:41.534
And she said, "They was good."

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.784
(laughter)

00:01:45.757 --> 00:01:47.952
He said, "No, no, no.

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:50.035
"Mother, you didn't eat those birds.

00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:52.579
"Those birds cost $5,000 apiece.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:53.897
"They could dance, they could sing,

00:01:53.897 --> 00:01:56.350
"they could talk," and she said,

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.209
"Well, they should have said something."

00:02:00.209 --> 00:02:02.731
You know, that's where we end up, too,

00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:03.664
if we don't speak up

00:02:03.664 --> 00:02:05.494
for what we believe.

00:02:05.494 --> 00:02:07.256
There is no one who doesn't

00:02:07.256 --> 00:02:09.068
have obstacles in their life.

00:02:09.068 --> 00:02:11.456
You know, even Bill Gates has obstacles.

00:02:11.456 --> 00:02:12.289
He's got to figure out

00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:13.555
how to keep all that money,

00:02:13.555 --> 00:02:17.313
so, you know, everybody's got problems.

00:02:17.313 --> 00:02:18.783
It really is how you

00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:20.980
face those problems.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.678
If you allow it to be

00:02:22.678 --> 00:02:24.961
an insurmountable wall

00:02:24.961 --> 00:02:27.211
then you become the victim.

00:02:28.378 --> 00:02:31.697
If you say this is a piece of cake

00:02:31.697 --> 00:02:33.692
I'm finding my way over, under,

00:02:33.692 --> 00:02:35.738
around, or through this obstacle

00:02:35.738 --> 00:02:37.290
one way or another,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.123
and you gear yourself to solutions

00:02:41.888 --> 00:02:43.971
that's always the answer.

00:02:44.942 --> 00:02:46.464
Always be thinking about

00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:49.081
how I can do something rather than

00:02:49.081 --> 00:02:51.164
why I can't do something?

00:02:52.097 --> 00:02:53.907
So many people lead

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:56.090
their lives by reacting

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.918
rather than by thinking,

00:02:57.918 --> 00:02:59.478
and really when you think about it

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:01.147
why do we have these

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:02.872
incredibly sophisticated brains

00:03:02.872 --> 00:03:05.116
with these big frontal lobes?

00:03:05.116 --> 00:03:07.417
Because we're the only creatures

00:03:07.417 --> 00:03:09.846
that God made with the ability

00:03:09.846 --> 00:03:12.126
to extract information

00:03:12.126 --> 00:03:13.875
from the past and the present,

00:03:13.875 --> 00:03:15.325
and to project it into

00:03:15.325 --> 00:03:17.158
a plan for the future,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.010
so we don't have to react.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.663
You know, animals they're just

00:03:21.663 --> 00:03:24.930
whatever is happening they react to it.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.388
Why is it that so many people

00:03:26.388 --> 00:03:27.785
act like animals?

00:03:27.785 --> 00:03:29.076
Why do they just react

00:03:29.076 --> 00:03:30.250
to what's going on

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.200
rather than to plan and strategize?

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.041
We have the ability to figure out

00:03:34.041 --> 00:03:36.949
where we want to be a year from now,

00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:38.220
five years from now,

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.281
10 years from now,

00:03:39.281 --> 00:03:41.150
and to begin to organize things

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.419
in a way in order to

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:45.095
accomplish those things,

00:03:45.095 --> 00:03:46.451
and when we don't do that

00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:49.059
we place ourselves in tremendous

00:03:49.059 --> 00:03:52.027
jeopardy because we place ourselves

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:55.749
in a situation where we can only react.

00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:57.706
I had a horrible temper,

00:03:57.706 --> 00:03:59.043
poor self-esteem.

00:03:59.043 --> 00:04:00.717
All the things that you

00:04:00.717 --> 00:04:03.605
would think would preclude success,

00:04:03.605 --> 00:04:05.261
but I had something very important.

00:04:05.261 --> 00:04:06.428
I had a mother

00:04:07.642 --> 00:04:09.412
who believed in me,

00:04:09.412 --> 00:04:11.867
and I had a mother who would

00:04:11.867 --> 00:04:13.911
never allow herself to be a victim

00:04:13.911 --> 00:04:15.537
no matter what happened.

00:04:15.537 --> 00:04:17.319
Never made excuses,

00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:19.232
and she never accepted

00:04:19.232 --> 00:04:21.054
an excuse from us.

00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:22.559
If we ever came up with an excuse

00:04:22.559 --> 00:04:24.286
she always said, "Do you have a brain?"

00:04:24.286 --> 00:04:27.211
If the answer was yes, then she said,

00:04:27.211 --> 00:04:28.044
"Then you could have thought

00:04:28.044 --> 00:04:28.877
"your way out of it."

00:04:28.877 --> 00:04:29.710
It doesn't matter what John,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:30.543
or Susan, or Mary,

00:04:30.543 --> 00:04:32.959
or anybody else did or said.

00:04:32.959 --> 00:04:34.215
It was the most important thing

00:04:34.215 --> 00:04:35.925
she did for my brother and myself

00:04:35.925 --> 00:04:38.722
because if you don't accept excuses

00:04:38.722 --> 00:04:40.897
pretty soon people stop giving them,

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:42.971
and they start looking for solutions.

00:04:42.971 --> 00:04:45.036
That is a critical issue

00:04:45.036 --> 00:04:47.734
when it comes to success.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:48.851
I would get on the bus,

00:04:48.851 --> 00:04:50.026
and I would go downtown

00:04:50.026 --> 00:04:51.612
to the Detroit Institute of Arts,

00:04:51.612 --> 00:04:52.891
and roam through those galleries

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:53.920
until I knew every painter

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.376
who painted a picture,

00:04:55.376 --> 00:04:56.596
and when they were born,

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:57.429
and when they died,

00:04:57.429 --> 00:04:58.827
and what period it represented,

00:04:58.827 --> 00:05:00.216
and I was always listening

00:05:00.216 --> 00:05:02.456
to my portable radio

00:05:02.456 --> 00:05:04.229
listening to classical music.

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:05.737
I'd be walking down the streets

00:05:05.737 --> 00:05:07.706
of Detroit listening to classical music,

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.621
and people thought I was nuts.

00:05:09.621 --> 00:05:10.656
This guy is crazy,

00:05:10.656 --> 00:05:12.631
but years later

00:05:12.631 --> 00:05:13.996
when I decided that

00:05:13.996 --> 00:05:15.482
I wanted to be a neurosurgeon

00:05:15.482 --> 00:05:17.020
I wanted to go to the place

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.618
that's best known for neurosurgery,

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.153
and that would be Johns Hopkins,

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.413
but as I said before

00:05:21.413 --> 00:05:23.115
they only took two people a year

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:24.587
out of 125 applicants,

00:05:24.587 --> 00:05:26.775
but when I got an interview,

00:05:26.775 --> 00:05:27.608
and I went there

00:05:27.608 --> 00:05:29.638
the fellow who was in charge of

00:05:29.638 --> 00:05:31.252
the Neurosurgery Residency Program.

00:05:31.252 --> 00:05:32.952
George B. Udvarhelyi,

00:05:32.952 --> 00:05:34.166
was also in charge of

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:37.612
Cultural Affairs at the hospital.

00:05:37.612 --> 00:05:38.859
Somehow the conversation

00:05:38.859 --> 00:05:40.275
turned to classical music,

00:05:40.275 --> 00:05:42.032
and we talked for over an hour

00:05:42.032 --> 00:05:43.502
about different composers,

00:05:43.502 --> 00:05:44.814
and their styles, conductors,

00:05:44.814 --> 00:05:46.477
orchestras, orchestral halls.

00:05:46.477 --> 00:05:47.310
There was no way he

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.445
wasn't taking me in the program

00:05:48.445 --> 00:05:49.736
because he had to have somebody

00:05:49.736 --> 00:05:51.255
to discuss these things with,

00:05:51.255 --> 00:05:52.799
but what I emphasize to

00:05:52.799 --> 00:05:54.782
young people all the time is

00:05:54.782 --> 00:05:56.257
there's no such thing

00:05:56.257 --> 00:05:57.866
as useless knowledge

00:05:57.866 --> 00:05:59.818
because you never know what doors

00:05:59.818 --> 00:06:02.068
it's going to open for you.

00:06:03.121 --> 00:06:04.488
The more you know

00:06:04.488 --> 00:06:06.835
the more options you have.

00:06:06.835 --> 00:06:08.591
Almost any accomplishment

00:06:08.591 --> 00:06:11.669
in the history of the world

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:12.755
has come at the hands of

00:06:12.755 --> 00:06:14.514
people who have taken risk.

00:06:14.514 --> 00:06:15.797
People who are willing

00:06:15.797 --> 00:06:17.222
to push the envelope.

00:06:17.222 --> 00:06:18.332
People who are willing

00:06:18.332 --> 00:06:20.168
to explore the unknown.

00:06:20.168 --> 00:06:23.626
Even if you go and you look in the Bible

00:06:23.626 --> 00:06:25.155
the people that are

00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:26.624
held up as the heroes

00:06:26.624 --> 00:06:28.076
they're not the ones who are sitting

00:06:28.076 --> 00:06:29.866
in their tent under an olive tree,

00:06:29.866 --> 00:06:31.254
you know, they were people who

00:06:31.254 --> 00:06:34.664
were going out there doing stuff.

00:06:34.664 --> 00:06:35.874
The key thing about

00:06:35.874 --> 00:06:37.898
taking risk is learning,

00:06:37.898 --> 00:06:39.323
and if something doesn't

00:06:39.323 --> 00:06:41.385
work out learn from it,

00:06:41.385 --> 00:06:43.193
and then you're able to move further.

00:06:43.193 --> 00:06:45.572
We have to always recognize that

00:06:45.572 --> 00:06:49.129
knowledge frequently comes at a price.

00:06:49.129 --> 00:06:51.052
Thomas Edison said he knew

00:06:51.052 --> 00:06:54.570
999 ways that a light bulb did not work.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.020
Many of us are familiar

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.845
with the cleaning Formula 409.

00:06:57.845 --> 00:06:59.242
Why did they call it that?

00:06:59.242 --> 00:07:01.045
The first 408 didn't work,

00:07:01.045 --> 00:07:02.827
but you just put these

00:07:02.827 --> 00:07:03.890
things into perspective,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:04.952
and you learn from them.

00:07:04.952 --> 00:07:07.295
Walter Dandy the great neurosurgeon

00:07:07.295 --> 00:07:09.155
at Johns Hopkins many decades ago

00:07:09.155 --> 00:07:11.024
was the first one to do operations

00:07:11.024 --> 00:07:12.340
on the posterior fossa

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:13.338
of the back part of the brain.

00:07:13.338 --> 00:07:15.493
No one thought it could be done.

00:07:15.493 --> 00:07:18.752
The first 13 patients all died.

00:07:18.752 --> 00:07:20.106
Now can you imagine how

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:21.717
devastating that was?

00:07:21.717 --> 00:07:23.138
I can't even imagine what he said

00:07:23.138 --> 00:07:24.775
to the 14th patient when they said,

00:07:24.775 --> 00:07:27.659
"How did the other 13 do?"

00:07:27.659 --> 00:07:28.732
The fact of the matter is

00:07:28.732 --> 00:07:31.414
we now are able to do that operation

00:07:31.414 --> 00:07:33.164
routinely and safely.

00:07:34.396 --> 00:07:36.063
I did one last week.

00:07:38.183 --> 00:07:39.691
We just have to put all these

00:07:39.691 --> 00:07:40.925
things in perspective,

00:07:40.925 --> 00:07:42.737
and recognize that we will

00:07:42.737 --> 00:07:44.198
never make progress

00:07:44.198 --> 00:07:47.146
unless we're willing to take risks,

00:07:47.146 --> 00:07:48.992
but we have to take them

00:07:48.992 --> 00:07:52.517
in an appropriate fashion because

00:07:52.517 --> 00:07:54.753
there's a large group of people

00:07:54.753 --> 00:07:55.979
who never get anywhere

00:07:55.979 --> 00:07:58.045
because they're afraid to take a risk.

00:07:58.045 --> 00:07:59.431
There's another group of people

00:07:59.431 --> 00:08:00.584
who never get anywhere because

00:08:00.584 --> 00:08:02.336
they take too many of the wrong risks.

00:08:02.336 --> 00:08:03.303
They're always getting

00:08:03.303 --> 00:08:04.653
not back into the pond,

00:08:04.653 --> 00:08:06.114
so that's where this

00:08:06.114 --> 00:08:07.761
tremendous brain comes in

00:08:07.761 --> 00:08:10.611
where you are able to analyze.

00:08:10.611 --> 00:08:11.444
One of the things

00:08:11.444 --> 00:08:13.525
that I hated was poverty.

00:08:13.525 --> 00:08:14.423
Some people hate spiders.

00:08:14.423 --> 00:08:16.055
Some people hate snakes.

00:08:16.055 --> 00:08:19.055
I hated poverty I couldn't stand it,

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:22.349
but, you know, my mother couldn't

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:23.276
stand the fact that

00:08:23.276 --> 00:08:24.907
we were doing poorly in school,

00:08:24.907 --> 00:08:26.432
and she prayed and she asked God

00:08:26.432 --> 00:08:27.268
to give her wisdom.

00:08:27.268 --> 00:08:28.101
What could she do to get her

00:08:28.101 --> 00:08:29.799
young sons to understand the importance

00:08:29.799 --> 00:08:31.364
of developing their minds,

00:08:31.364 --> 00:08:33.388
so that they control their own lives,

00:08:33.388 --> 00:08:34.457
and you know what?

00:08:34.457 --> 00:08:35.623
God gave her the wisdom

00:08:35.623 --> 00:08:36.985
at least in her opinion.

00:08:36.985 --> 00:08:37.818
My brother and I didn't think

00:08:37.818 --> 00:08:39.318
it was that wise because

00:08:39.318 --> 00:08:41.279
it was to turn off the TV.

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:42.523
Let us watch only two or three

00:08:42.523 --> 00:08:43.805
TV programs during the week.

00:08:43.805 --> 00:08:45.034
With all that spare time

00:08:45.034 --> 00:08:46.202
read two books apiece from

00:08:46.202 --> 00:08:47.489
the Detroit Public Library,

00:08:47.489 --> 00:08:49.284
and submit to her written book reports,

00:08:49.284 --> 00:08:50.673
which she couldn't read,

00:08:50.673 --> 00:08:52.673
but we didn't know that.

00:08:54.039 --> 00:08:55.797
She put check marks and highlights,

00:08:55.797 --> 00:08:56.630
and stuff,

00:08:57.638 --> 00:09:00.388
but, you know, I just hated this.

00:09:01.876 --> 00:09:03.262
My friends were out having a good time.

00:09:03.262 --> 00:09:05.118
Her friends would criticize her.

00:09:05.118 --> 00:09:05.951
They would say,

00:09:05.951 --> 00:09:06.784
"You can't make boys stay in the

00:09:06.784 --> 00:09:07.627
"house reading books.

00:09:07.627 --> 00:09:09.452
"They'll grow up they'll hate you."

00:09:09.452 --> 00:09:10.790
I would overhear them and I'd say,

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:11.699
"Mother, you know they're right,"

00:09:11.699 --> 00:09:13.980
but she didn't care, you know,

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.017
but after a while I actually

00:09:16.017 --> 00:09:19.326
began to enjoy reading those books

00:09:19.326 --> 00:09:20.485
because we were very poor,

00:09:20.485 --> 00:09:21.842
but between the covers of those books

00:09:21.842 --> 00:09:22.762
I could go anywhere,

00:09:22.762 --> 00:09:23.595
I could be anybody,

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:25.156
I could do anything.

00:09:25.156 --> 00:09:26.945
I bean to read about people

00:09:26.945 --> 00:09:29.104
of great accomplishment.

00:09:29.104 --> 00:09:30.286
As I read those stories

00:09:30.286 --> 00:09:33.203
I began to see a connecting thread.

00:09:34.069 --> 00:09:35.748
I began to see that the person

00:09:35.748 --> 00:09:37.143
who has the most to do with you,

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:40.454
and what happens to you in life is you.

00:09:40.454 --> 00:09:42.218
You make decisions.

00:09:42.218 --> 00:09:43.770
You decide how much energy

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.880
you want to put behind that decision.

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.327
I came to understand that I had

00:09:48.327 --> 00:09:50.617
control of my own destiny.

00:09:50.617 --> 00:09:52.126
At that point I didn't hate

00:09:52.126 --> 00:09:53.683
poverty anymore because

00:09:53.683 --> 00:09:55.267
I knew it was only temporary.

00:09:55.267 --> 00:09:56.990
I knew I could change that.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.820
It was incredibly liberating for me.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:00.953
It made all the difference.

00:10:00.953 --> 00:10:01.985
You know, the human brain

00:10:01.985 --> 00:10:04.825
if you have an average brain

00:10:04.825 --> 00:10:07.782
you're capable of almost anything

00:10:07.782 --> 00:10:10.576
because of the complexity of our brains.

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:13.055
Billions and billions of neurons.

00:10:13.055 --> 00:10:14.047
Hundreds of billions

00:10:14.047 --> 00:10:15.449
of interconnections.

00:10:15.449 --> 00:10:16.782
It can process more than

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:18.314
two million bits of information

00:10:18.314 --> 00:10:20.168
in one second.

00:10:20.168 --> 00:10:21.783
It never forgets anything

00:10:21.783 --> 00:10:22.616
you've ever seen,

00:10:22.616 --> 00:10:24.488
anything you've ever heard.

00:10:24.488 --> 00:10:26.648
You know, with something like that

00:10:26.648 --> 00:10:27.992
sitting up here

00:10:27.992 --> 00:10:29.040
why would you ever

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.466
utter the words I can't?

00:10:31.466 --> 00:10:33.093
- What do you mean by Think Big?

00:10:33.093 --> 00:10:34.492
- Each one of those letters

00:10:34.492 --> 00:10:36.206
means something special.

00:10:36.206 --> 00:10:37.639
The "T" is for talent

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:39.287
which God gives to everybody,

00:10:39.287 --> 00:10:41.455
and everybody has their own

00:10:41.455 --> 00:10:43.201
unique set of talents.

00:10:43.201 --> 00:10:45.573
Develop those, use them,

00:10:45.573 --> 00:10:48.760
and let those carry you where they will.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.616
The "H" is for honesty.

00:10:50.616 --> 00:10:51.782
If you live a clean and honest life,

00:10:51.782 --> 00:10:53.289
and you don't have a bunch

00:10:53.289 --> 00:10:54.721
of skeletons in the closet

00:10:54.721 --> 00:10:57.072
it's really hard for them to come up

00:10:57.072 --> 00:10:58.499
if you don't put them in there,

00:10:58.499 --> 00:11:00.519
and it really uncomplicates

00:11:00.519 --> 00:11:03.139
your life quite substantially.

00:11:03.139 --> 00:11:05.466
The "I" is for insight,

00:11:05.466 --> 00:11:06.855
which comes from listening to people

00:11:06.855 --> 00:11:07.688
who have already gone

00:11:07.688 --> 00:11:09.731
where you're trying to go.

00:11:09.731 --> 00:11:12.900
A lot of the adults in your life

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.156
have been through the very things that

00:11:15.156 --> 00:11:17.041
you're going through right now,

00:11:17.041 --> 00:11:18.996
and if you can talk to them,

00:11:18.996 --> 00:11:21.113
and see what lessons they learned

00:11:21.113 --> 00:11:22.418
maybe you don't have to

00:11:22.418 --> 00:11:23.939
repeat the same things.

00:11:23.939 --> 00:11:26.073
Maybe that will allow you to move

00:11:26.073 --> 00:11:29.645
much further and faster than they did.

00:11:29.645 --> 00:11:30.908
The "N" is for nice.

00:11:30.908 --> 00:11:32.531
Be nice to people because

00:11:32.531 --> 00:11:33.986
once they get over their suspicion

00:11:33.986 --> 00:11:34.819
of why you're being nice

00:11:34.819 --> 00:11:36.051
they'll be nice to you,

00:11:36.051 --> 00:11:37.739
and you can get so much more done

00:11:37.739 --> 00:11:38.977
when you're being nice,

00:11:38.977 --> 00:11:40.529
when they're being nice,

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:42.189
and not get caught up

00:11:42.189 --> 00:11:45.106
in the meanness and the selfishness

00:11:46.291 --> 00:11:47.548
that is promoted by many

00:11:47.548 --> 00:11:50.243
segments of our society.

00:11:50.243 --> 00:11:51.570
The "K" is for knowledge,

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.501
which is the way that you

00:11:53.501 --> 00:11:55.450
make yourself more valuable,

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:58.494
and no one else can do that for you.

00:11:58.494 --> 00:12:01.186
You have to actively participate

00:12:01.186 --> 00:12:02.282
in the process of

00:12:02.282 --> 00:12:04.114
becoming knowledgeable.

00:12:04.114 --> 00:12:06.671
The mechanism for doing that

00:12:06.671 --> 00:12:09.615
is the next letter "B" books.

00:12:09.615 --> 00:12:10.503
You know, some people say

00:12:10.503 --> 00:12:12.285
"I don't like reading books.

00:12:12.285 --> 00:12:14.456
"Besides that I can learn anything

00:12:14.456 --> 00:12:15.703
"I need to learn from watching

00:12:15.703 --> 00:12:17.136
"DVD's and videos."

00:12:17.136 --> 00:12:20.256
That's like saying that you can

00:12:20.256 --> 00:12:21.593
develop your muscles by watching

00:12:21.593 --> 00:12:23.283
somebody else lift weights.

00:12:23.283 --> 00:12:24.805
It doesn't work that way.

00:12:24.805 --> 00:12:26.604
You have to exercise your brain

00:12:26.604 --> 00:12:29.027
if you want it to work for you.

00:12:29.027 --> 00:12:32.048
Reading is a perfect way of doing that.

00:12:32.048 --> 00:12:35.474
The second "I" is for in-depth learning.

00:12:35.474 --> 00:12:36.499
Learning for the sake of

00:12:36.499 --> 00:12:38.136
knowledge and understanding

00:12:38.136 --> 00:12:40.380
as opposed to just learning some

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.578
answers so that you can

00:12:41.578 --> 00:12:43.241
put them down on a quiz.

00:12:43.241 --> 00:12:44.854
That superficial learning

00:12:44.854 --> 00:12:47.331
doesn't last very long.

00:12:47.331 --> 00:12:50.193
The last letter "G" is for God,

00:12:50.193 --> 00:12:52.254
which is such an important part

00:12:52.254 --> 00:12:55.067
of each of us as individuals,

00:12:55.067 --> 00:12:57.484
and the fabric of our nation.

00:12:58.854 --> 00:13:01.965
We make a grave error when we

00:13:01.965 --> 00:13:04.641
allow the secular progressive

00:13:04.641 --> 00:13:07.395
movement to push God out of our lives.

00:13:07.395 --> 00:13:11.140
We shouldn't try to force God on them,

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.697
but by the same token

00:13:12.697 --> 00:13:14.029
we should not allow them

00:13:14.029 --> 00:13:16.880
to remove God from our lives.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:17.976
- [Interviewer] Maybe some parting

00:13:17.976 --> 00:13:19.436
words for our viewers.

00:13:19.436 --> 00:13:22.257
- Well, my parting words would involve

00:13:22.257 --> 00:13:24.344
what do you really want in your life?

00:13:24.344 --> 00:13:26.272
What do you want to accomplish?

00:13:26.272 --> 00:13:27.660
Are you complacent,

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.018
and are you happy just to be doing

00:13:30.018 --> 00:13:31.454
what you're doing,

00:13:31.454 --> 00:13:32.287
or do you think

00:13:32.287 --> 00:13:33.820
there's potentially more?

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:35.418
If you think there's more

00:13:35.418 --> 00:13:38.254
why not use that tremendous brain

00:13:38.254 --> 00:13:39.754
that God gave you

00:13:40.683 --> 00:13:42.699
in order not only to envision it,

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:44.695
but to make it happen

00:13:44.695 --> 00:13:46.331
not only for yourself,

00:13:46.331 --> 00:13:48.291
but for those people around you

00:13:48.291 --> 00:13:50.302
because real success involves

00:13:50.302 --> 00:13:52.670
lifting up those who are around you,

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.267
and making your environment

00:13:54.267 --> 00:13:55.680
a better place than it was

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.430
before you got there.

00:13:59.752 --> 00:14:01.277
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:01.277 --> 00:14:02.190
I made this video because

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.249
Rachel Alley asked me to,

00:14:04.249 --> 00:14:05.677
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:05.677 --> 00:14:07.097
you want me to profile next

00:14:07.097 --> 00:14:08.849
leave it down in the comments below,

00:14:08.849 --> 00:14:10.138
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:10.138 --> 00:14:10.971
I'd also love to know

00:14:10.971 --> 00:14:12.411
which of Ben's top 10 rules

00:14:12.411 --> 00:14:13.571
hit you the hardest,

00:14:13.571 --> 00:14:15.011
had the biggest meaning for you?

00:14:15.011 --> 00:14:16.377
Leave it in the comments,

00:14:16.377 --> 00:14:18.122
and I will join in the discussion.

00:14:18.122 --> 00:14:19.875
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:19.875 --> 00:14:21.278
Continue to believe,

00:14:21.278 --> 00:14:23.111
and I'll see you soon.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.796
- What are you particularly gifted in?

00:14:31.796 --> 00:14:33.847
Do you have any special

00:14:33.847 --> 00:14:35.517
gifts and talents?

00:14:35.517 --> 00:14:36.709
You know, everybody has special

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:38.231
gifts and talents.

00:14:38.231 --> 00:14:40.830
I started analyzing my own life,

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:43.165
and I said what really

00:14:43.165 --> 00:14:46.396
unusual things have happened to you

00:14:46.396 --> 00:14:48.285
because usually those very

00:14:48.285 --> 00:14:49.795
unusual things have something

00:14:49.795 --> 00:14:52.024
to do with your gifts and talents.

00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:53.096
I remembered that

00:14:53.096 --> 00:14:55.429
I became a Foosball champion

00:14:56.782 --> 00:14:57.699
in college.

00:14:58.878 --> 00:15:00.087
Go off to a big prestigious

00:15:00.087 --> 00:15:01.735
Ivy League school like Yale,

00:15:01.735 --> 00:15:02.568
but I remembered

00:15:02.568 --> 00:15:04.059
being a Foosball champion.

00:15:04.059 --> 00:15:05.874
That was a great thing,

00:15:05.874 --> 00:15:07.540
but that required a tremendous

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:10.589
amount of eye-hand coordination.

00:15:10.589 --> 00:15:13.163
I remember working one summer

00:15:13.163 --> 00:15:15.309
at a steel factory,

00:15:15.309 --> 00:15:18.108
and as a college student

00:15:18.108 --> 00:15:20.693
they let me operate the crane.

00:15:20.693 --> 00:15:23.297
Now these cranes are very complex.

00:15:23.297 --> 00:15:24.516
They had a whole bunch of levers

00:15:24.516 --> 00:15:26.845
that you had to push and pull

00:15:26.845 --> 00:15:28.872
because you were maneuvering

00:15:28.872 --> 00:15:32.043
these multi-ton steel packages

00:15:32.043 --> 00:15:33.949
through very narrow aisles,

00:15:33.949 --> 00:15:35.081
and stacking them

00:15:35.081 --> 00:15:37.335
on the backs of trucks.

00:15:37.335 --> 00:15:40.102
If you made even the slightest error

00:15:40.102 --> 00:15:41.484
you could make a multiple

00:15:41.484 --> 00:15:43.818
thousand dollar error.

00:15:43.818 --> 00:15:46.302
These people trusted me having never

00:15:46.302 --> 00:15:48.599
worked one of these things before

00:15:48.599 --> 00:15:49.984
after seeing me play with it

00:15:49.984 --> 00:15:51.901
for just an hour or so,

00:15:53.019 --> 00:15:54.695
and I had that job for the whole summer.

00:15:54.695 --> 00:15:56.157
I said, "Those people must have had

00:15:56.157 --> 00:15:58.994
"a tremendous amount of confidence

00:15:58.994 --> 00:16:00.566
"in me a college student

00:16:00.566 --> 00:16:01.626
"who was the only college student

00:16:01.626 --> 00:16:05.024
"working at the steel factory."

00:16:05.024 --> 00:16:06.774
So I started thinking

00:16:08.032 --> 00:16:09.306
I want to do something

00:16:09.306 --> 00:16:10.657
that requires a lot

00:16:10.657 --> 00:16:13.251
of eye-hand coordination.

00:16:13.251 --> 00:16:14.501
Then I was also

00:16:15.992 --> 00:16:17.574
a very careful person.

00:16:17.574 --> 00:16:18.896
I never knocked things over,

00:16:18.896 --> 00:16:20.474
and said, "Oops,"

00:16:20.474 --> 00:16:22.220
which is a great characteristic

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:23.982
for a neurosurgeon I got to tell you.

00:16:23.982 --> 00:16:26.232
(laughter)

00:16:27.575 --> 00:16:29.041
I had the ability to think

00:16:29.041 --> 00:16:30.708
in three-dimensions.

00:16:31.779 --> 00:16:33.270
Most people think in two-dimensions,

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:34.588
but to think in three-dimensions

00:16:34.588 --> 00:16:35.421
to be able to keep your

00:16:35.421 --> 00:16:38.091
spatial orientation very, very

00:16:38.091 --> 00:16:39.924
important if you're in somebody's brain

00:16:39.924 --> 00:16:42.162
because it's just like mush.

00:16:42.162 --> 00:16:43.834
It's just a big pile of mush

00:16:43.834 --> 00:16:46.006
that doesn't have a lot of structures

00:16:46.006 --> 00:16:48.163
in it to tell you where you are,

00:16:48.163 --> 00:16:50.945
so if you can't keep in mind

00:16:50.945 --> 00:16:52.682
where all the little nuclei,

00:16:52.682 --> 00:16:55.412
and tracks and things are

00:16:55.412 --> 00:16:57.229
all of your patients will come out

00:16:57.229 --> 00:16:58.488
looking like that,

00:16:58.488 --> 00:17:00.398
so you don't necessarily

00:17:00.398 --> 00:17:02.315
want to do that either.

00:17:04.122 --> 00:17:06.485
Then I loved to dissect things.

00:17:06.485 --> 00:17:08.087
I always loved to dissect things.

00:17:08.087 --> 00:17:09.684
You know, when I was a kid,

00:17:09.684 --> 00:17:10.517
and I would be sitting

00:17:10.517 --> 00:17:12.157
at the table eating

00:17:12.157 --> 00:17:14.858
I would have my fork and my knife out

00:17:14.858 --> 00:17:16.399
dissecting out all the little

00:17:16.399 --> 00:17:18.839
neurovascular bundles out of the meat,

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:21.148
and putting that in a pile.

00:17:21.148 --> 00:17:22.294
My mother would be saying,

00:17:22.294 --> 00:17:23.127
"You can't eat with us.

00:17:23.127 --> 00:17:25.441
"You go sit on the landing."

00:17:25.441 --> 00:17:27.568
(laughter)

00:17:27.568 --> 00:17:28.821
Always, if there was

00:17:28.821 --> 00:17:32.179
a little bug around or a little

00:17:32.179 --> 00:17:34.823
rodent or something that I could catch

00:17:34.823 --> 00:17:36.815
I always knew what was inside.

00:17:36.815 --> 00:17:38.548
I loved to dissect things,

00:17:38.548 --> 00:17:40.425
so I put all that together,

00:17:40.425 --> 00:17:41.377
and I said,

00:17:41.377 --> 00:17:44.294
"You'd be a terrific neurosurgeon."

00:17:46.063 --> 00:17:47.278
It doesn't mean that I couldn't

00:17:47.278 --> 00:17:49.140
have been a good dentist,

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:50.975
or a history professor,

00:17:50.975 --> 00:17:53.808
or even, God forbid, a lawyer.

00:17:53.808 --> 00:17:56.058
(laughter)

00:17:58.879 --> 00:18:01.766
I probably would not have been

00:18:01.766 --> 00:18:03.413
excelling to the same degree

00:18:03.413 --> 00:18:07.425
because I wouldn't have taken advantage

00:18:07.425 --> 00:18:09.177
of the special gifts and talents

00:18:09.177 --> 00:18:10.219
that God had provided.

